===============================================================================
REACTIVE RULE ENGINE  --  HWUT 2.0 Behavioural Trace Validator
DESIGN DOCUMENT
===============================================================================

NAME
  REACTIVE RULE ENGINE. Temporal properties are expressed as event-triggered, 
  guarded reactions.

COMPANION FILES
  SYNTAX.txt        Concrete syntax of rule files and event traces.
  DISCUSSIONS.txt   Running design log: resolved decisions, rationales, 
                    and rejected alternatives.

VOCABULARY
  TIME LINE     The sequence of runtime input events (TRACE) and the engine's 
                internal product.
  TRACE         Concrete input time line parsed at run time.
  RULE FILES    Plain-text rules authored by a human.
  TRANSPILER    Python tool that parses rule files, executes static analysis, 
                and emits the Luau ENGINE.
  ENGINE        The generated standalone Luau application that processes a trace 
                and prints a report.
  OBJECT SPACE  The Luau table space containing all event, object, and lurker 
                instances at run time.
  LURKER        A declared, parameterized, dormant-until-armed rule unit.
  FOCUS         An environment where only one lurker can be active at a time; 
                activating a new one disables the current.
  REPORT        The deterministic block of text printed by the engine for HWUT 
                nominal recording comparison.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PURPOSE AND SHAPE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The component produces a deterministic textual report of observed behavior 
  for validation against a nominal recording via the HWUT harness.

  THE TWO STAGES:
    1. TRANSPILE: Python parses the rule files, runs static analysis, and emits 
       a complete, self-contained Luau application.
    2. RUN: The generated Luau engine reads a trace from a stream or file, 
       evaluates the rules, and writes its output to stdout. 
       The trace is purely runtime input and is never compiled into the 
       application.

  CORE CONSTRAINT
    Portability. The transpiler is pure Python. The engine is generated 
    Luau executing on a standard Luau interpreter. Per-event 
    processing never crosses a language or process boundary.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. PIPELINE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    RULE FILES (authored by a human)
        |
        |  STAGE 1 -- TRANSPILE (Python, once)
        ├── parse rule files
        ├── static analysis (cascade graph, name resolution)
        └── emit standalone Luau application
        v
    ENGINE (generated Luau application)
        |
        |  STAGE 2 -- RUN (Luau, once per trace)
        ├── TRACE input (ends with explicit END event)
        ├── evaluate each event in temporal order:
        │     ├── advance synthetic CLOCKs and emit pending events
        │     ├── fire matching consequence rules
        │     ├── fire live lurker instances
        │     └── expand cascades at T and mutate objects
        └── collect and process output streams
        v
    OUTPUT
        ├── '##' debug stream: printed live during run (emission order)
        └── REPORT block: printed after completion (sorted and deduplicated)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE MODEL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EVENTS
    Ephemeral struct-like aggregates with typed fields. They exist only 
    at the instant they occur. 'time' and 'dt' (delta since the previous 
    event) are intrinsic fields of type 'number'. The pair (type, time) 
    uniquely identifies an occurrence.

  OBJECTS
    Stateful aggregates persisting across time spans. Defined in the 
    SETUP block using Luau via the 'Class.create' factory.

  TIME LINE BOUNDS -- BEGIN AND END
    Traces are framed by two implicit events: BEGIN fires immediately before the 
    first trace event; END fires immediately after the last. 
    An input trace must end with an explicit END event, otherwise the engine 
    rejects the trace. ANY matches both boundary events.

  TRACE TIMING
    A trace must be uniformly EXPLICITLY-TIMED or CLOCK-TIMED; modes cannot 
    be mixed. The engine never consults a system clock; 'dt' 
    is always derived as the delta to the previous event's 'time'.

  CLOCKS (Synthetic Time Generation)
    The engine supports internal periodic event generation via CLOCKs. 
    A CLOCK emits a specific event at a defined interval of synthetic time. 
    It is evaluated whenever an external trace event arrives, calculating 
    `delta_t` and emitting all pending clock events linearly up to the current 
    trace event timestamp.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ARCHITECTURE -- TRANSPILER AND ENGINE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CONTROL PLANE (Parsed by Transpiler)
    Declarations, rule headers, triggers, and body verbs (=>, => +, => "...") 
    utilizing statically literal event-type and lurker names.

  DATA PLANE (Verbatim Luau text carried into Engine)
    Member-function bodies, state-changing mutation blocks '{ }', guard 
    expressions, and the SETUP body.

  THE ONE-OBJECT-SPACE INVARIANT
    There is exactly one object model: the Luau table space of the running 
    engine process. Every event, object, and lurker instance exists 
    exclusively there. 

  IDENTITY & METATABLES
    Events, Lurkers, and Foci are identified by transpiler-generated real 
    metatables. Objects are constructed at SETUP time via the 'Class.create' 
    factory. 

  SANDBOXING & CAPABILITIES
    The transpiler emits only the specific capabilities granted by the test 
    configuration. The generated bootstrap injects 'Class', 'tracer', 
    the metatables, and the predefined Comparator classes (Glob, Approx, 
    Less, Eq, Pattern, etc.) for query matching.

  WATCHDOG LIMITS
    Luau execution arising from rule logic is bounded by a maximum instruction 
    count configuration parameter enforced via the Luau VM count hook.

  SOURCE MAPPING
    The transpiler must emit Luau code carrying line directives or comments 
    mapping back to the originating rule-file 'file:line'.

  LUA HELPERS (Transpile-time companions to Python)

    Located in the 'lua/' subdirectory:
      find_end.lua  -- Persistent daemon resolving opaque Luau spans.
      analyze.lua   -- One-shot batch tool executing reference analysis.
      bootstrap.lua -- Runtime sandbox, factories, and value classes.
      run.lua       -- Runtime engine harness and trace firing loop.
      aux.lua       -- Shared Luau lexer / parser utilities.

    See also: lua/README.txt

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. CASCADES, TERMINATION, AND STATIC ANALYSIS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE TERMINATION GUARANTEE
    Within a single instant T, a given event type may occur at most once. 
    Cascades monotonically consume the finite set of declared event types, 
    guaranteeing termination even in cyclic graphs.

  STATIC ANALYSIS CHECKS (Executed in Python via analyze.lua data)
    - CHECK 1: CASCADE DEPTH (Warning). Computes the longest simple path in 
      the cascade graph.
    - CHECK 2: NAME RESOLUTION (Fatal). Verifies all event, lurker, and field 
      names against declarations. Executes a sound guard read-only check 
      (rejecting assignments or mutating builtins like table.insert). Unresolved 
      names halt compilation.
    - CHECK 3: REACHABILITY (Warning). Flags any rule whose trigger event type 
      is unreachable, or any lurker that is never armed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. RULES -- CONSEQUENCES, LURKERS, AND THE TRACER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CONSEQUENCES
    Syntax: 'on <trigger> [ & <guard> ] => <consequence>+ off'. 
    Body items include event emissions (`=>`), lurker arming (`=> +`), 
    report strings (`=> "..."`), or state mutations (`=> { ... }`). 

  LURKER LIFECYCLE
    - Arming: `=> + LURKER(params)` instantiates a class. Identity is defined 
      by the parameter list. Duplicate armings of an existing identity are 
      silent no-ops.
    - Stamped State: The engine intrinsically stamps every instance with 
      `lurker.begin_time` and `lurker.begin_event_index`.
    - Execution Bindings: Fired rules inside a lurker expose `event` and 
      `lurker` bindings.
    - Hooks: Optional `construct { ... }` runs once on creation. 
      Optional `destruct { ... }` runs once when an 'until' condition triggers 
      or trace completes.
    - Global Lurker: An internal singleton processes top-level rules.

  POLYMORPHIC QUERIES (Lurkers and Events)
    Both Lurker instances and traced Event histories share a unified query 
    interface routed through a generated `_match` dispatcher:
    - Lists: `:list(conditions)`
    - Booleans: `:has()`, `:any()`, `:all()`, `:none()`, `:empty()`
    - Temporal: `:last()`, `:since()`
    Conditions accept literal values or injected Comparator classes (`Glob()`, 
    `Approx()`, etc.).

  THE TRACER
    Events can only be queried if configured in the SETUP block:
      `tracer.watch(EVENT, [by=key], [last=count])`
    Querying an untraced event type, or using incompatible key attributes, 
    triggers a transpile-time FATAL error.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. THE REPORT AND LOGGING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  REPORT ASSEMBLY (`=> "..."`)
    Report strings are evaluated using Luau string interpolation. Lines are 
    buffered during the execution path. Upon trace completion, the entire 
    block is alphabetically sorted and uniqueness-filtered (deduplicated) to 
    ensure absolute output determinism.

  THE '##' DEBUG STREAM
    Standard Luau `print()` statements are intercepted live during execution, 
    automatically prefixed with '##', and pushed immediately to stdout. They 
    are ignored during HWUT nominal comparison.

  THE DIAGNOSTIC LOG
    The engine maintains an internal, bounded circular buffer log recording every 
    fired rule with its corresponding 'file:line' mapping and timestamp. 

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. ENCODING PATTERNS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Temporal operators are replaced by evaluation arithmetic over the intrinsic 
  `time` and `dt` fields within standard boolean guards:
    - BOUNDED RESPONSE: Arm a lurker on event X; evaluate violations against 
      `lurker.begin_time + N`; clear early via `until Y`.
    - ABSENCE: Evaluate a deadline with a boolean flag state.
    - DEBOUNCE: Resolve via conflicting `until` clauses tracking the success 
      condition and the timeout boundary.
    - MUTUAL EXCLUSION: Guarded check on `ANY` event type.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. INTEGRATION SURFACE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TRANSPILER INTERFACE (Python)
    `transpile(rule_files, capability_config) -> luau_application | errors`

  ENGINE INTERFACE (Luau)
    Executed via system call feeding a trace stream. Output maps chronological 
    `##` debug lines first, followed by the finalized deterministic REPORT block.

===============================================================================
